# Canon

Full design doc: `docs/DESIGN.md`. Knowledge base: `docs/kb/README.md`.

## Pillars

1. **Movement and ability selection are the same verb.** Rolling to a cell decides what is armed
   on arrival (the up face). Nothing else selects abilities.
2. **Geometry is grammar.** A shape's form predicts its behavior with zero tutorialization, and
   must never lie.
3. **The world is white; color is memory.** Color is always earned, never decorative.
4. **Teach through the body.** Rules are learned physically before they are ever named in text.

## Invariants — breaking one is a regression no matter what it adds

- **The quarter-turn invariant.** One quarter-turn per cell. Orientation in sim is an integer
  `0..23`; roll and pivot are proven table lookups. Quaternions exist only in render.
- **The sim/render boundary.** Sim is pure and deterministic at fixed 120Hz with seeded RNG. Render
  reads snapshots and never mutates them. A run is `(seed, inputLog)`.
- **Socket proofs.** Every seal ships a BFS proof over `(cell × orientation)` asserting
  `solveMoves > arriveMoves` and `solveMoves ≤ arriveMoves + 6`.
- **Camera policy.** Camera consumes *linear* roll progress and *resting* ground height, never the
  eased position. **Traversal never shakes the camera** — shake is impact-only, ≥ 0.05, labelled
  `// impact:`.
- **Terrain rules.** Integer heights only. A slope is a staircase. A cliff (|Δh| ≥ 2) is a wall
  both ways. Never sub-cell height.
- **Collision data ≠ render data.** Occupancy is a component query; meshes are a render-side
  mapping.
- **Zero allocation in per-tick and per-frame hot paths.**
- **Water is Wallace's substance.** Look and behavior match
  https://madebyevan.com/webgl-water/ — heightfield, cube volume coupling, above/below refraction,
  Jacobian caustics, visual buoyancy. Lattice xz/ori and camera rest-height do not follow the
  waves. Details: `.cursor/rules/water.mdc`.

## Working rules

- **Prove, don't spot-check.** If a claim is provable, ship the proof.
- **Every fix ships a guard encoding the *mechanism* that found it**, not merely the fix.
- **Replay the exact defect before patching it**, then add that replay to the corpus.
- **A flaky test is a real bug or an incident assertion.** Never retry-to-green.
- Determinism hazards: `Math.sin/cos/pow/exp` are not spec-pinned across JS engines. Never call
  them in `sim`.
